.TH CVECTOR_RESERVE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cvector_reserve \- reserve capacity for future elements
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

bool cvector_reserve(cvec v, size_t new_capacity_count);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates capacity for new_capacity_count elements or more, rounding the
capacity up to the nearest power of two (minimum 4). Reserving the
expected size in advance helps you avoid many reallocations.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v                    Vector to reserve capacity for
new_capacity_count   Minimum number of elements to reserve
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns true when the reservation succeeds, and also when the capacity
is already large enough
Returns false in three cases: a failed allocation, a capacity above
ccol_max_elem_count, and a capacity that overflows a size_t after the
multiplication by the element size
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Does nothing when the requested capacity <= the current capacity
The real capacity can be larger, because of the rounding to a power
of 2
The function tests both limits in RETURN VALUE against the rounded-up
capacity rather than against new_capacity_count itself, so a request
that is inside a limit can still fail after the rounding
A vector of wide elements reaches the size_t overflow limit first: that
limit bounds the capacity at SIZE_MAX divided by the element size, which
for an element wider than one byte is the lower of the two
Does not change the element count
Asserts if v is NULL
The cvec_reserve(3) macro evaluates new_capacity_count exactly once,
on the failure path too, so an expression with a side effect is safe
to give
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_reserve (3)
